passwords.txt — where it comes from, and its licence (ADR 0083 item 3)

The 10 000 commonest passwords of 12 characters or more, lower-cased, one
a line, in the order of the list below: the passwords that the server's
floor of 12 characters lets through, and so the ones worth refusing.

Source: SecLists, Passwords/Common-Credentials/xato-net-10-million-passwords-1000000.txt
  (Mark Burnett's ten million passwords of 2015, published at xato.net:
  the one million commonest, by frequency)
  https://github.com/danielmiessler/SecLists/blob/3a5f62f0e18a1b8a36b25399aff0077f3706e5af/Passwords/Common-Credentials/xato-net-10-million-passwords-1000000.txt
  Repository commit: 3a5f62f0e18a1b8a36b25399aff0077f3706e5af (master,
  2026-10-13T11:17:17Z); the file's last change: c205c36a445bff37f8e58a9ec829105cd4975c58
  (2025-05-08, moved to Common-Credentials).
  Fetched on 2026-10-13; the fetched file's SHA-256:
  424a3e03a17df0a2bc2b3ca749d81b04e79d59cb7aeec8876a5a3f308d0caf51

Made with (POSIX shell):
  LC_ALL=C awk 'length($0) >= 12 { print tolower($0) }' xato-net-10-million-passwords-1000000.txt \
    | awk '!seen[$0]++' | head -n 10000 > passwords.txt
passwords.txt's SHA-256:
  ec8c0a3e04cb76025fbb33469bd8e5b35def58cc913649e548237ffed657a4eb

Why not SecLists' top 10 000 of the same list
(xato-net-10-million-passwords-10000.txt): 24 of its 10 000 have 12
characters or more, and neither password1234 nor qwertyuiop12 is among
them; the floor already refuses the other 9 976.

